menu "espOS SignalK"

    config ESPOS_SK_RX_FRAME_MAX
        int "Largest inbound stream frame kept (bytes)"
        default 16384
        range 2048 131072
        help
            Delta messages larger than this are dropped (logged). A
            subscription burst after reconnect (values + meta for many
            paths) can reach several KiB; raise on PSRAM boards that
            subscribe to whole families.

    config ESPOS_SK_TLS
        bool "Talk to the server over TLS (https/wss)"
        default n
        select MBEDTLS_CERTIFICATE_BUNDLE
        help
            Compile the https/wss transports, switchable per install with
            the sk.tls setting (off by default, so enabling this changes
            nothing until somebody asks for it).

            Off by default because espOS is a boat-LAN device and the OTA
            path -- the one that actually matters -- is protected by image
            signatures rather than by transport security. What TLS buys
            here is keeping the SignalK access token off the wire, which
            starts to matter on a shared marina network or as soon as a
            server is reachable from outside the boat.

            Flash cost is close to nothing in a default espOS build:
            mbedTLS and the certificate bundle are already linked because
            signed OTA goes through esp_https_ota (measured on esp32c6,
            same image size with and without). What it does cost is
            roughly 20 KB of RAM while the stream connection is open.

            The server certificate is verified against the bundled
            Mozilla roots; there is no way yet to pin a private CA, so a
            self-signed server certificate will be refused.

    config ESPOS_SK_NOTIFICATIONS
        bool "Device-health notifications"
        default y
        help
            Let the firmware raise SignalK notifications under
            notifications.espos.<label>.<key> for conditions it can judge
            itself -- memory pressure, a dependency that has gone away --
            via espos_sk_notify().

            Without them such a condition surfaces as a device that has
            quietly stopped working, which looks the same as a hardware
            fault and is the expensive kind of problem to diagnose.

            Turn it off on parts where the RAM matters more: the table is
            allocated on first use, so an unused build already costs
            nothing, but disabling also drops the code and the periodic
            low-memory check.

    config ESPOS_SK_MAX_NOTIFY
        int "Distinct notification keys"
        depends on ESPOS_SK_NOTIFICATIONS
        default 8
        range 1 32
        help
            Slots for remembered notification state, one per key. Allocated
            together on the first notification (~128 bytes each), never
            freed. A firmware's set of conditions is fixed at build time,
            so size this to what it actually raises.

endmenu
